# Your Turn #3

Let $X_1, ..., X_n$ be i.i.d. random variables with a finite expected value $E[X_i] = \mu$ and $\mathbb{V}(X_i) = \sigma^2 < \infty$.

## Question 1

Show that $\mathbb{E}[S_n^2] = \mathbb{E}[X^2] - \mathbb{E}[\bar{X_n}^2]$.

*Answer:*

\begin{align}
\mathbb{E}[S_n^2] &= \mathbb{E} \left[ \frac{1}{n} \sum_{i=1}^n (X_i - \bar{X_n})^2 \right] \\
&= \mathbb{E} \left[ \frac{1}{n} \sum_{i=1}^n X_i^2 - \frac{1}{n} 2 \sum_{i=1}^n X_i \bar{X_n} + \frac{1}{n} \sum_{i=1}^n \bar{X_n}^2 \right] \\
&= \frac{1}{n} \sum_{i=1}^n \mathbb{E}[X_i^2] - \mathbb{E} \left[ \frac{1}{n} 2\bar{X_n} \sum_{i=1}^n X_i \right] + \mathbb{E} \left[ \bar{X_n}^2 \right] \\
&= \mathbb{E} \left[ X^2 \right] - \mathbb{E} \left[ \frac{1}{n} 2\bar{X_n} n \bar{X_n} \right] + \mathbb{E} \left[ \bar{X_n}^2 \right] \\
&= \mathbb{E} \left[ X^2 \right] - 2\mathbb{E} \left[\bar{X_n}^2 \right] + \mathbb{E} \left[ \bar{X_n}^2 \right] \\
&= \mathbb{E} \left[ X^2 \right] - \mathbb{E} \left[\bar{X_n}^2 \right]
\end{align}

## Question 2

Show that $\mathbb{E}[X^2] = \sigma^2 + \mu^2$. Hint: recall the formula for the variance using expectations.

*Answer:*

Recall that $\mathbb{V} (X) = \mathbb{E} \left[ X^2 \right] - \mathbb{E} [X]^2$. We therefore have:

\begin{align}
\mathbb{E} \left[ X^2 \right] &= \mathbb{V} (X) + \mathbb{E} [X]^2 \\
&= \sigma^2 + \mu^2
\end{align}

## Question 3

Show that $\mathbb{E}[\bar{X_n}^2] = \frac{\sigma^2}{n} + \mu^2$. *Hint: the formula for $\mathbb{V}(\bar{X_n})$ will be useful.*

*Answer:*

Using the variance formula and replacing $X$ by $\bar{X_n}$, we have:

\begin{align}
\mathbb{E} \left[ \bar{X_n}^2 \right] &= \mathbb{V} (\bar{X_n}) + \mathbb{E} \left[ \bar{X_n} \right]^2 \\
&= \frac{\sigma^2}{n} + \mu^2
\end{align}

## Question 4

Combine the above results to show that $\mathbb{E}[S_n^2] \neq \sigma^2$.

*Answer:*

We can use the results from questions 2 and 3:

\begin{align}
\mathbb{E} \left[ S_n^2 \right] &= \mathbb{E} \left[ X^2 \right] - \mathbb{E} \left[ \bar{X_n}^2 \right] \\
&= \sigma^2 + \mu^2 - (\frac{\sigma^2}{n} + \mu^2) \\
&= \sigma^2 - \frac{\sigma^2}{n} \\
&= \frac{n-1}{n} \sigma^2 \\
&\neq \sigma^2
\end{align}

## Question 5

Load this [voter data](https://www.dropbox.com/scl/fi/lnz14t3a2cj6kw0l217yo/pumpernickel_voter.csv?rlkey=4dupeb3v1ibxfb1y6283tq403&dl=1). Use `R` to compute the variance of Pumpernickel voters: (1) manually (i.e., taking the sum of deviations from the mean), and (2) using the `var` command. What do you notice?

*Answer:*



```{r}
#| message: false
#| warning: false

library(tidyverse)

voter_data <- read.csv("https://www.dropbox.com/scl/fi/lnz14t3a2cj6kw0l217yo/pumpernickel_voter.csv?rlkey=4dupeb3v1ibxfb1y6283tq403&dl=1")

voter_data |> 
  mutate(deviation_from_mean = pumpernickel_voter - mean(pumpernickel_voter)) |> 
  summarise(var_manual = 1/n() * sum(deviation_from_mean^2),
            var_command = var(pumpernickel_voter),
            var_manual_adjusted = n()/(n()-1) * var_manual)
```



